---
/**
 * FIG. 00 — Identification.
 *
 * A node map: six paths around one body, in three planes, each path drawn as
 * the arc its traveller has just swept rather than a closed ring.
 *
 * Geometry, all in the 430x244 viewBox, body at (176,118):
 *
 *   path  plane   rx   ry   flat    k   side  tilt  near  node  tail  period
 *   N-01    A    112   77   1.45   46    L    -10°  63.5   6.0  bare    52s
 *   N-02    B    100   34   2.94   40    R    +26°  30.8   5.4   44%    44s
 *   N-03    A     78   54   1.44   30    L    -10°  45.7   4.6   68%    30s
 *   N-04    B     66   28   2.36   24    R    +26°  25.6   4.0  bare    24s
 *   N-05    C     58   50   1.16   12    R    -40°  45.6   3.4   62%    19s
 *   N-06    A     50   34   1.47   16    L    -10°  30.6   3.0  full    16s
 *
 * The plane column is the organising idea. Every path used to share one
 * flattening and one tilt, so the figure read as a single ring system seen from
 * directly above. Here the three planes are held apart by how foreshortened
 * they are — A is open at 1.45:1, B is nearly edge-on at 2.94:1, C is almost
 * face-on at 1.16:1 — and by tilts 36° and 66° apart. Within a plane the paths
 * share a tilt and a side and nest strictly by both semi-axes, so they never
 * cross each other; all the crossings in the figure are between planes, which
 * is what makes them legible as crossings rather than as clutter.
 *
 * `k` is how far the body sits off a path's centre, `near` the closest the path
 * gets to it, solved numerically over the tilted curve. Placing the body on each
 * ellipse's true focus instead ties the offset to the flattening — 1.45:1 would
 * force e = 0.73 — and at that eccentricity every path is an egg and all six
 * dive to within 24 units of the body at once. Setting `k` independently costs
 * the focus and buys both back: the nearest path clears the body's r = 12 by 14
 * units, so it sits in a clearing. The gap shows in the axis ticks too, which
 * are the true crossings: none falls between 141 and 210.
 *
 * Periods follow Kepler's third law against rx — T proportional to rx^1.5,
 * normalised so the outermost runs 52s — so the index is a real table.
 *
 * The tail column is a composition decision, not a technical one. Six trailing
 * arcs read as busy no matter how they are placed, and the reference sheet does
 * not draw one per node either: its called-out node is a lone dot with only its
 * leader attached. N-01 is that dot here, and N-04 is the second bare one, so
 * four arcs carry the structure and two travellers move unaccompanied.
 *
 * Each path's tilt is baked into the arc command's x-axis-rotation rather than
 * a wrapping <g transform>, so every element shares one coordinate frame. That
 * is what lets the N-01 callout ride the same path as its traveller and still
 * hold its label upright.
 *
 * The figure is pure CSS. src/scripts/orbit.ts only pauses it when it scrolls
 * out of view, which is worth 76 ms/s of main thread — see the note there, and
 * docs/perf-home-renders.md for what the cheaper animation mechanics cost in
 * design terms.
 */
---

<header class="zone head">
  <div class="head-id">
    <p class="p-u p-tag zh">Identification · Fig. 00</p>
    <h1><span>Martin Sundal</span><span class="sur">Aspås</span></h1>
    <p class="role">
      <b>Software engineer.</b> Robotics and simulation, out of Trondheim.
    </p>

    <div class="meta">
      <div class="p-stack">
        <p class="p-u p-u--hair">Field</p>
        <p class="p-u p-u--ink">Robotics</p>
        <p class="p-u p-u--ink">Simulation</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-stack">
        <p class="p-u p-u--hair">Location</p>
        <p class="p-u p-u--ink">Trondheim</p>
        <p class="p-u p-u--ink">Norway · 63.4°N</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-stack">
        <p class="p-u p-u--hair">Specimens</p>
        <p class="p-u p-u--ink">05 projects</p>
        <p class="p-u p-u--ink">01 board</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="orb-wrap" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="orb ins">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 430 244">
        <!-- Reference axes. They overrun the field on every side; the heavy cap
             at the top of the vertical is the only weighted line out here. -->
        <path class="h ax" d="M2 118h326M176 6v232"></path>
        <path class="i cap" d="M176 6v19"></path>

        <!-- Field boundary, clear of every path. Sized nearer the outer two it
             ran alongside them and read as a seventh path in the wrong pen. -->
        <ellipse class="dotted" cx="161" cy="126" rx="152" ry="88"></ellipse>

        <!-- Four of the six paths are stroked, as a trailing arc that ends
             exactly on its traveller: pathLength="100" puts the dash pattern in
             percent of the path, and both the dash offset and the traveller are
             driven off the same --sweep, so they cannot drift apart. N-01 and
             N-04 have no arc at all — their travellers ride --path unaccompanied
             — which is why they are absent here rather than hidden. -->
        <path
          class="trail o2"
          pathLength="100"
          d="M301.8 179.4A100 34 26 1 1 122.1 91.7A100 34 26 1 1 301.8 179.4"
        ></path>
        <path
          class="trail o3"
          pathLength="100"
          d="M223.3 109.7A78 54 -10 1 1 69.6 136.8A78 54 -10 1 1 223.3 109.7"
        ></path>
        <path
          class="trail o5"
          pathLength="100"
          d="M229.6 73A58 50 -40 1 1 140.8 147.6A58 50 -40 1 1 229.6 73"></path>
        <path
          class="trail solid o6"
          pathLength="100"
          d="M209.5 112.1A50 34 -10 1 1 111 129.5A50 34 -10 1 1 209.5 112.1"
        ></path>

        <!-- Where the paths actually cross the horizontal. -->
        <g class="f">
          <circle cx="22.6" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
          <circle cx="71" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
          <circle cx="111.8" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
          <circle cx="130.5" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
          <circle cx="141.2" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
          <circle cx="209.8" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
          <circle cx="223.8" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
          <circle cx="233.8" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
          <circle cx="237.7" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
          <circle cx="241.8" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
          <circle cx="250.7" cy="118" r="0.9"></circle>
        </g>

        <!-- The body the whole field is arranged around. -->
        <circle class="f" cx="176" cy="118" r="12"></circle>

        <circle class="rides trav o1" r="6"></circle>
        <circle class="rides trav o2" r="5.4"></circle>
        <circle class="rides trav o3" r="4.6"></circle>
        <circle class="rides trav o4" r="4"></circle>
        <circle class="rides trav o5" r="3.4"></circle>
        <circle class="rides trav o6" r="3"></circle>

        <!-- The callout rides N-01 too, so it stays hitched to the traveller it
             names instead of pointing at empty space. -->
        <g class="rides callout o1">
          <path class="h" d="M0 0l26-30h30"></path>
          <text class="t-ink" x="62" y="-27">N-01</text>
        </g>

        <!-- Registration. -->
        <path class="h reg" d="M49 40h10M54 35v10M301 222h10M306 217v10"></path>

        <!-- Index of paths: identifier, plane, and the period its traveller
             actually runs at. -->
        <g class="idx">
          <path class="h" d="M340 34v14M340 208v14"></path>
          <circle class="h" cx="356" cy="50" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="f" cx="356" cy="50" r="3.4"></circle>
          <g data-orbit="o1">
            <text x="368" y="53">N-01</text>
            <text class="t-ink" x="398" y="53">A</text>
            <text x="410" y="53">52s</text>
          </g>
          <circle class="f" cx="356" cy="82" r="3.0"></circle>
          <g data-orbit="o2">
            <text x="368" y="85">N-02</text>
            <text class="t-ink" x="398" y="85">B</text>
            <text x="410" y="85">44s</text>
          </g>
          <circle class="f" cx="356" cy="114" r="2.6"></circle>
          <g data-orbit="o3">
            <text x="368" y="117">N-03</text>
            <text class="t-ink" x="398" y="117">A</text>
            <text x="410" y="117">30s</text>
          </g>
          <circle class="f" cx="356" cy="146" r="2.3"></circle>
          <g data-orbit="o4">
            <text x="368" y="149">N-04</text>
            <text class="t-ink" x="398" y="149">B</text>
            <text x="410" y="149">24s</text>
          </g>
          <circle class="f" cx="356" cy="178" r="2.0"></circle>
          <g data-orbit="o5">
            <text x="368" y="181">N-05</text>
            <text class="t-ink" x="398" y="181">C</text>
            <text x="410" y="181">19s</text>
          </g>
          <circle class="f" cx="356" cy="210" r="1.8"></circle>
          <g data-orbit="o6">
            <text x="368" y="213">N-06</text>
            <text class="t-ink" x="398" y="213">A</text>
            <text x="410" y="213">16s</text>
          </g>
        </g>
      </svg>
    </div>
    <p class="p-u">
      Fig. 00 · Orbital synthesis · Node map · 06 paths · 03 planes
    </p>
  </div>
</header>

<script>
  import "../../scripts/orbit.ts";
</script>

<style>
  .head {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, auto);
    gap: clamp(28px, 4vw, 72px);
    align-items: center;
    margin-bottom: var(--loose);
  }

  .head-id {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  h1 {
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 8.2vw, 6.4rem);
    line-height: 0.88;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin-top: 16px;
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  h1 span {
    display: block;
  }

  h1 .sur {
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .role {
    margin-top: 22px;
    font-size: clamp(15px, 1.5vw, 19px);
    line-height: 1.35;
    max-width: 30ch;
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
  }

  .role b {
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .meta {
    display: flex;
    gap: clamp(18px, 3.4vw, 46px);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 30px;
    padding-top: 9px;
    border-top: var(--rule) solid var(--hair);
  }

  .orb-wrap {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: flex-end;
    gap: 9px;
    justify-self: end;
  }

  .orb {
    width: clamp(268px, 38vw, 520px);
    aspect-ratio: 430 / 244;
  }

  .orb svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }

  /* Paused whenever the masthead is off screen. CSS animations keep ticking
     when their element scrolls away, and this one is expensive: a registered
     custom property that two paint-level properties then calc() off, so every
     frame is a style recalc and a repaint of the whole figure. Nothing about
     the drawing changes here — the animations simply stop when there is nobody
     to see them, and resume where they left off. */
  .orb[data-still] .trail,
  .orb[data-still] .rides {
    animation-play-state: paused;
  }

  /* The axes are the faintest thing in the figure — they locate it, they are
     not part of it. */
  .orb .ax {
    opacity: 0.55;
  }

  .orb .cap {
    stroke-width: 2.2;
  }

  /* Registration marks are the quietest thing on the plate — they hold the
     corners without asking to be read. */
  .orb .reg {
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .orb .idx text {
    font-size: 7.5px;
  }

  .orb .dotted {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--hair);
    stroke-width: 1.6;
    stroke-dasharray: 0.1 6.5;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    vector-effect: non-scaling-stroke;
  }

  /* --sweep is the single clock for a path. Registering it is what makes it
     interpolate smoothly rather than jump at each keyframe, and it drives both
     the traveller's position along the path and the dash offset of the arc
     behind it — so the arc always ends exactly on its traveller, with nothing
     to keep in sync by hand. */
  @property --sweep {
    syntax: "<number>";
    inherits: false;
    initial-value: 0;
  }

  /* Every path is drawn as a trailing arc rather than a closed ring: with
     pathLength="100" the dash pattern is in percent of the path, so a --tail of
     42 shows 42% of it, ending at --sweep. Six closed rings is most of what made
     the field look like a tangle; six arcs put roughly half as much ink on it.

     N-02's --tail is 42 exactly, and so is one parameter in every other figure
     on the plate that has numbers in it. There are six. */
  .orb .trail {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--hair);
    /* No vector-effect here, deliberately, and it is the one rule in this file
       that cannot be made consistent with the rest of the plate. non-scaling-
       stroke makes the browser stroke in device space while pathLength
       normalises in user space, so the dash pattern and the path disagree by
       the render scale — at 520px wide that is 1.21x, which walked every arc
       about a fifth of an orbit clear of the traveller it is supposed to end
       on. Without it the stroke scales with the figure instead: 0.85 lands near
       a 1px hairline at the desktop size and thins gracefully below that. */
    stroke-width: 0.85;
    stroke-dasharray: var(--tail) calc(100 - var(--tail));
    stroke-dashoffset: calc(var(--tail) - var(--sweep));
    animation: orb-sweep linear infinite;
  }

  .orb .solid {
    stroke-dasharray: none;
  }

  /* Anything that rides a path is drawn at the origin and carried there by
     offset-path — the offset translate composes with the element's own geometry
     rather than replacing it, so a traveller must have no cx/cy of its own.

     offset-anchor defaults to the element's transform-origin, and for a <g> that
     resolves against its own bounding box — so the callout, whose box sits up
     and to the right of its leader's start, landed a leader's length off its
     traveller. Pinning both to the view-box origin makes the offset a plain
     translate to the path point for every rider, whatever its shape.
     offset-rotate is pinned too, so the callout's label stays upright instead of
     tumbling round its orbit. */
  .orb .rides {
    transform-box: view-box;
    transform-origin: 0 0;
    offset-path: var(--path);
    offset-distance: calc(var(--sweep) * 1%);
    offset-anchor: 0 0;
    offset-rotate: 0deg;
    animation: orb-sweep linear infinite;
  }

  .orb .trav {
    fill: var(--ink);
  }

  /* Three planes. A is open, B nearly edge-on, C almost face-on; paths sharing a
     plane share a tilt and never cross one another.

     --sweep here is where the path sits at load, and each delay is that value
     run back through the inverse of the orb-sweep easing — so the figure opens
     in exactly the arrangement it falls back to when kernel.css collapses every
     animation under reduced motion. The values are spread as widely as the paths
     allow, except N-01's: it is parked where its callout has clear air above the
     field, since that is the frame everyone sees first. */
  .orb .o1 {
    --path: path(
      "M241 106.5A112 77 -10 1 1 20.4 145.4A112 77 -10 1 1 241 106.5"
    );
    --sweep: 64;
    animation-duration: 52s;
    animation-delay: -38.05s;
  }

  .orb .o2 {
    --path: path(
      "M301.8 179.4A100 34 26 1 1 122.1 91.7A100 34 26 1 1 301.8 179.4"
    );
    --tail: 42;
    --sweep: 2;
    animation-duration: 44s;
    animation-delay: -0.41s;
  }

  .orb .o3 {
    --path: path(
      "M223.3 109.7A78 54 -10 1 1 69.6 136.8A78 54 -10 1 1 223.3 109.7"
    );
    --tail: 68;
    --sweep: 42;
    animation-duration: 30s;
    animation-delay: -10.53s;
  }

  .orb .o4 {
    --path: path(
      "M256.9 157.5A66 28 26 1 1 138.3 99.6A66 28 26 1 1 256.9 157.5"
    );
    --sweep: 88;
    animation-duration: 24s;
    animation-delay: -22.49s;
  }

  .orb .o5 {
    --path: path("M229.6 73A58 50 -40 1 1 140.8 147.6A58 50 -40 1 1 229.6 73");
    --tail: 62;
    --sweep: 74;
    animation-duration: 19s;
    animation-delay: -15.87s;
  }

  .orb .o6 {
    --path: path(
      "M209.5 112.1A50 34 -10 1 1 111 129.5A50 34 -10 1 1 209.5 112.1"
    );
    --tail: 100;
    --sweep: 24;
    animation-duration: 16s;
    animation-delay: -2.36s;
  }

  /* Kepler's second law, near enough to read as one: the path starts at
     perihelion, so the first half decelerates out to aphelion at 50% and the
     second accelerates back. The two curves are mirrors, which is what keeps the
     speed continuous across the loop instead of jerking at 0%. Peak is about 5x
     the crawl. */
  @keyframes orb-sweep {
    0% {
      --sweep: 0;
      animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.25, 0.55, 0.6, 0.82);
    }
    50% {
      --sweep: 50;
      animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(0.4, 0.18, 0.75, 0.45);
    }
    100% {
      --sweep: 100;
    }
  }

  /* The offset-distance and animation-delay on each path are one decision, not
     two. The distances were picked to spread the travellers as widely as their
     paths allow, and each delay is that distance run back through the inverse of
     the orb-travel easing — so the figure loads in exactly the arrangement it
     falls back to when kernel.css collapses every animation under reduced
     motion. Either way it opens as a system, never as a conjunction. N-01's is
     the one not chosen purely for spread: it is parked where its callout has
     clear air above the field, since that is the frame everyone sees first. */

  @media (max-width: 67rem) {
    .head {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 38px;
    }
    .orb-wrap {
      align-items: flex-start;
      justify-self: start;
    }
    .orb {
      width: min(520px, 90vw);
    }
  }

  /* Centred with the rest of the figures on a phone. The caption under it stays
     flush left — it is lettering on the sheet, not a title for the drawing. */
  @media (max-width: 46rem) {
    .orb {
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
